// Sections 37-48: structs, unions, and enums.

render_section_37 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A struct is a nominal record type: a fixed set of named fields, with an
        \\identity that comes from its name rather than from its shape. It is
        \\introduced by an ordinary constant declaration whose value is a `struct`
        \\body, so the declaration form is `Name :: struct { ... }`.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Vec2 :: struct {
        \\    x: s64 = 1;
        \\    y: s64 = 2;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\Every member ends with a semicolon, and the diagnostic names the offending
        \\member: a comma gives `error: struct member 'x' must end with ';'`. A
        \\member is a terminated declaration rather than an element of a list because
        \\it may carry a default expression, and that expression can itself contain
        \\commas. `union` and `enum` members are terminated the same way, with the
        \\same wording.
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The closing brace ends the declaration and nothing may follow it: a trailing
        \\semicolon at file scope is `error: bad decl name ';'`. A struct may be
        \\declared at file scope or inside a procedure body, and a procedure-local
        \\declaration keeps its plain name, so `type_name` on a local `Local` reports
        \\`Local`.
    );
    subheading(4, "Field defaults");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\A field may carry `= expr` after its annotation. The expression is an
        \\ordinary runtime expression, not a compile-time constant, and it is
        \\evaluated afresh on every default initialization of the struct. A field
        \\with no default is zeroed.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\counter: s64 = 0;
        \\next_id :: () -> s64 { counter += 1; return counter; }
        \\
        \\Entry :: struct {
        \\    id:   s64 = next_id();
        \\    name: string = "anon";
        \\}
        \\
        \\Slot :: struct { x: s64 = 7; y: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Entry;
        \\    b: Entry;
        \\    print("fresh defaults: % %\n", a.id, b.id);
        \\    print("string default: %\n", a.name);
        \\
        \\    s: Slot;
        \\    print("declared: % %\n", s.x, s.y);
        \\
        \\    u: Slot = #undefined;
        \\    u.x = 1;
        \\    u.y = 2;
        \\    print("undefined then set: % %\n", u.x, u.y);
        \\
        \\    row: [2]Slot;
        \\    print("array element: % %\n", row[0].x, row[0].y);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\fresh defaults: 1 2
        \\string default: anon
        \\declared: 7 0
        \\undefined then set: 1 2
        \\array element: 0 0
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\`a` and `b` receive different identifiers because `next_id()` runs twice.
        \\`u: Slot = #undefined;` skips initialization entirely, so neither the
        \\default nor the zeroing happens. A default cannot name a sibling field:
        \\`b: s64 = a + 1;` is `error: unbound 'a'`, because the defaults are checked
        \\in the enclosing scope and a field name is not a binding there.
    );
    note(9, #string,oneline
        \\Field defaults are not applied to the elements of an array. `row: [2]Slot;`
        \\gives `row[0].x == 0`, not `7`. An array of structs is zeroed as a block.
        \\Where the defaults matter, initialize the elements from a literal.
    );
    subheading(10, "Generic fields");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\A field annotation containing `$T` introduces an inferred type parameter
        \\for the whole struct. Later fields spell it as plain `T`. The struct is
        \\then instantiated by applying it to type arguments, and each instantiation
        \\is a separate type with its own mangled name.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Pair :: struct {
        \\    first:  $T;
        \\    second: T;
        \\    label:  string = "pair";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Pair(s64) = .{ .first = 1, .second = 2 };
        \\    q: Pair(f64) = .{ 1.5, 2.5 };
        \\    print("% % %\n", p.first, p.second, p.label);
        \\    print("% %\n", q.first, q.second);
        \\    print("%\n", type_name(type_of(p)));
        \\    print("%\n", type_name(type_of(q)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\1 2 pair
        \\1.5 2.5
        \\Pair_int
        \\Pair_f64
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\`Pair_int` and `Pair_f64` are unrelated types: `q: Pair(f64) = p;` is
        \\`error: got Pair_int, expected Pair_f64`. An applied generic cannot carry a
        \\qualified literal, because the qualified form only accepts a bare
        \\identifier: `Pair(f64).{ 1.5, 2.5 }` is
        \\`error: struct literal needs a type`. Annotate the binding and use the
        \\inferred form instead.
    );
    subheading(15, "The two literal forms");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\A struct value is written with a brace literal. The qualified form names
        \\the type; the inferred form leaves the type to context. Both accept
        \\positional elements, named elements `.field = value`, or a mix, and both
        \\accept fewer elements than the struct has fields, in which case the
        \\remaining fields take their defaults.
    );
    table_header(17, "Form   —   Type comes from   —   Restriction");
    table_row(18, "`T.{ ... }`   |   The written name `T`.   |   `T` must be a bare identifier.");
    table_row(19, "`.{ ... }`   |   The expected type at that position.   |   There must be one.");
    table_row(20, "`T(A).{ ... }`   |   Nothing; rejected.   |   `error: struct literal needs a type`");
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Vec2 :: struct { x: s64 = 1; y: s64 = 2; }
        \\
        \\take :: (v: Vec2) -> s64 { return v.x + v.y; }
        \\make :: () -> Vec2 { return .{ 6, 7 }; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    w := Vec2.{ 10, 20 };
        \\    u := Vec2.{ .y = 7 };
        \\    m := Vec2.{ 3, .y = 4 };
        \\    z: Vec2 = .{ .x = 5, .y = 6 };
        \\    print("qualified positional: % %\n", w.x, w.y);
        \\    print("qualified named:      % %\n", u.x, u.y);
        \\    print("mixed:                % %\n", m.x, m.y);
        \\    print("inferred:             % %\n", z.x, z.y);
        \\
        \\    z = .{ 8, 9 };
        \\    print("assigned:  % %\n", z.x, z.y);
        \\    print("argument:  %\n", take(.{ 3, 4 }));
        \\    r := make();
        \\    print("returned:  % %\n", r.x, r.y);
        \\    grid: [2]Vec2 = .[ .{ 1, 1 }, .{ 2, 2 } ];
        \\    print("in array:  % %\n", grid[0].x, grid[1].y);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\qualified positional: 10 20
        \\qualified named:      1 7
        \\mixed:                3 4
        \\inferred:             5 6
        \\assigned:  8 9
        \\argument:  7
        \\returned:  6 7
        \\in array:  1 2
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\The inferred form works wherever the checker already has a concrete
        \\expectation: an annotated declaration, the left side of an assignment, an
        \\argument position, a `return`, and an element of an array literal whose
        \\element type is known. Without an expectation it is
        \\`error: struct literal needs a type`, which is what `v := .{ 1, 2 };`
        \\produces.
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Positional elements follow declaration order, which is not the order the
        \\fields are stored in. The layout rule is covered in the chapter on struct
        \\memory layout; the point here is that a literal is written against the
        \\source, never against the record.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Plain :: struct { a: u8; b: s64; c: u16; }
        \\
        \\offset :: (base: *$T, field: *$U) -> u64 {
        \\    return cast(u64)field - cast(u64)base;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := Plain.{ 1, 2, 3 };
        \\    print("positional: a=% b=% c=%\n", p.a, p.b, p.c);
        \\    print("offsets:     a=% b=% c=%\n",
        \\          offset(*p, *p.a), offset(*p, *p.b), offset(*p, *p.c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\positional: a=1 b=2 c=3
        \\offsets:     a=10 b=0 c=8
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Two literal mistakes have their own wording. Supplying more positional
        \\elements than the struct has fields is `error: no field '' in struct
        \\literal for V` (the empty name is the missing field it tried to fill), and
        \\a named element that does not match any field is `error: no field 'z' in
        \\struct literal for V`. Reading an absent field off a value is a different
        \\diagnostic, `error: no field 'z' in 'V'`, and it carries a
        \\`help: did you mean 'x'?` suggestion.
    );
    subheading(28, "Recursive structs");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\A struct is a value, so it cannot contain itself by value: the size would
        \\not be finite. Recursion goes through an indirection whose size does not
        \\depend on the pointee, which means `*T`, `?*T`, `[*]T`, a view, or a
        \\dynamic array.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Node :: struct {
        \\    value: s64;
        \\    next:  ?*Node;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    tail: Node = .{ .value = 2, .next = .None };
        \\    head: Node = .{ .value = 1, .next = *tail };
        \\    #pattern .Some(p) := head.next else { print("end\n"); return; };
        \\    print("% %\n", head.value, p.*.value);
        \\    print("size_of(Node)=%\n", size_of(Node));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\1 2
        \\size_of(Node)=16
    );
    note(32, #string,oneline
        \\Direct by-value recursion is not diagnosed. `Node :: struct { next: Node; }`
        \\type-checks and then overflows the compiler's own stack while the layout is
        \\computed, reporting only `thread 'main' has overflowed its stack`. Read a
        \\crash with no `error:` line in a file that declares a self-containing
        \\aggregate as this case.
    );
    subheading(33, "What a struct body may not contain");
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\A nested named type declaration. `Inner :: struct { ... }` written inside a
        \\struct body is `error: unexpected token '::'`. An anonymous aggregate
        \\written directly as a field type is the supported spelling; see the chapter
        \\on inline aggregate field types.
    );
    bullet(35, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` field. `V :: struct { a: s64; b: void; }` is `error: a zero-sized
        \\'void' member inside a struct, tuple, or array is not supported yet; remove
        \\the member, or wrap it in a single-field struct`, because erasing the member
        \\would renumber the declaration-order field indices that reflection reports.
    );
    bullet(36, #string,oneline
        \\Nothing at all is fine: `Empty :: struct { }` is a legal type of size `0`.
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\Directives written between `struct` and the opening brace change layout,
        \\generate instances, or rewrite type parameters. `#c_call` and `#packed` are
        \\covered in the chapter on struct memory layout, `#derive` in the chapter on
        \\deriving instances, and `#modify` in the chapter on function and aggregate
        \\directives.
    );
}

render_section_38 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An undecorated struct does not store its fields in declaration order. The
        \\compiler sorts them before assigning offsets, so that a record needs no
        \\internal padding beyond what the field alignments themselves require.
        \\Declaration order still governs everything a program writes: positional
        \\literals and the field indices reflection reports are both in source order,
        \\and a directive can pin the record to source order as well.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The sort is a single stable ordering with three keys, applied to the stored
        \\fields only. Placing the strictest alignments first means every following
        \\field lands on a boundary it already satisfies, and ordering by decreasing
        \\size within one alignment class packs the tail densely.
    );
    table_header(2, "Key   —   Direction");
    table_row(3, "Field alignment   |   Descending.");
    table_row(4, "Field size   |   Descending.");
    table_row(5, "Declaration index   |   Ascending, as the tiebreak.");
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Bytes :: struct { a: u8; b: [3]u8; c: u8; }
        \\Tie   :: struct { a: u32; b: u32; }
        \\Mixed :: struct { flag: bool; id: u64; code: u16; ratio: f32; }
        \\
        \\offset :: (base: *$T, field: *$U) -> u64 {
        \\    return cast(u64)field - cast(u64)base;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    x: Bytes; y: Tie; m: Mixed;
        \\    print("Bytes size=% a=% b=% c=%\n", size_of(Bytes),
        \\          offset(*x, *x.a), offset(*x, *x.b), offset(*x, *x.c));
        \\    print("Tie   size=% a=% b=%\n", size_of(Tie),
        \\          offset(*y, *y.a), offset(*y, *y.b));
        \\    print("Mixed size=% id=% ratio=% code=% flag=%\n", size_of(Mixed),
        \\          offset(*m, *m.id), offset(*m, *m.ratio),
        \\          offset(*m, *m.code), offset(*m, *m.flag));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\Bytes size=5 a=3 b=0 c=4
        \\Tie   size=8 a=0 b=4
        \\Mixed size=16 id=0 ratio=8 code=12 flag=14
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\`Bytes` shows the size key on its own: every field has alignment `1`, so
        \\the three-byte array goes first and `a` and `c` follow in declaration
        \\order. `Tie` shows the tiebreak: identical alignment and identical size,
        \\so the declared order is kept. `Mixed` shows the whole rule: `8`, `4`, `2`,
        \\`1`, giving `16` bytes with a single padding byte at the end, against the
        \\`24` bytes the declared order would have needed.
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The record's alignment is the largest field alignment, and its size is the
        \\end of the last field rounded up to that alignment. A struct with no fields
        \\has size `0`.
    );
    subheading(10, "Measuring a layout");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\There is no `offset_of` and no `align_of`. Offsets are obtained by
        \\subtracting the address of the value from the address of the field, and
        \\alignment is read from the type's `Type_Info` record. A generic helper
        \\keeps this to one line per field.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\offset :: (base: *$T, field: *$U) -> u64 {
        \\    return cast(u64)field - cast(u64)base;
        \\}
    );
    table_header(13, "Spelling   —   Result");
    table_row(14, "`size_of(T)`   |   The record size in bytes.");
    table_row(15, "`type_info(T).alignment`   |   The record alignment in bytes.");
    table_row(16, "`align_of(T)`   |   `error: unknown function align_of`");
    table_row(17, "`offset_of(T, \"b\")`   |   `error: unknown function offset_of`");
    table_row(18, "`struct #align(16) { ... }`   |   `error: unexpected token 'align'`");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\A declaration therefore cannot raise a record's alignment: it is always the
        \\maximum of the field alignments, or `1`. The two directives below are the
        \\whole layout vocabulary, and neither of them adds alignment.
    );
    subheading(20, "`#c_call`: declaration order and the C ABI");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\`struct #c_call { ... }` suppresses the sort. The fields are laid out in
        \\declaration order with the padding the target's C compiler would insert,
        \\which is what a record shared with C requires: the two sides must agree on
        \\offsets, and only the declared order is common to both languages.
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The directive also makes the compiler check that every field type has a
        \\C ABI at all. `V :: struct #c_call { r: c64; }` is `error: primitive c64 has
        \\no declared C ABI; pass its real and imaginary components separately`, and
        \\the check recurses into nested aggregates. The rest of the boundary is
        \\covered in the chapter on C data across the boundary.
    );
    subheading(23, "`#packed`: no padding");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`struct #packed { ... }` also suppresses the sort, and additionally removes
        \\all padding: the size is the plain sum of the field sizes and the record
        \\alignment is `1`. Writing both directives is accepted, and `#packed` wins,
        \\because it is the stronger constraint.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Plain  :: struct         { a: u8; b: u64; c: u8; }
        \\C_Aln  :: struct #c_call { a: u8; b: u64; c: u8; }
        \\Packed :: struct #packed { a: u8; b: u64; c: u8; }
        \\
        \\offset :: (base: *$T, field: *$U) -> u64 {
        \\    return cast(u64)field - cast(u64)base;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Plain; q: C_Aln; r: Packed;
        \\    print("Plain  size=% align=% a=% b=% c=%\n", size_of(Plain),
        \\          type_info(Plain).alignment,
        \\          offset(*p, *p.a), offset(*p, *p.b), offset(*p, *p.c));
        \\    print("C_Aln  size=% align=% a=% b=% c=%\n", size_of(C_Aln),
        \\          type_info(C_Aln).alignment,
        \\          offset(*q, *q.a), offset(*q, *q.b), offset(*q, *q.c));
        \\    print("Packed size=% align=% a=% b=% c=%\n", size_of(Packed),
        \\          type_info(Packed).alignment,
        \\          offset(*r, *r.a), offset(*r, *r.b), offset(*r, *r.c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\Plain  size=16 align=8 a=8 b=0 c=9
        \\C_Aln  size=24 align=8 a=0 b=8 c=16
        \\Packed size=10 align=1 a=0 b=1 c=9
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Three layouts of one declaration. `Plain` stores `b` at offset `0` although
        \\it is written second, and fits in `16` bytes. `C_Aln` keeps the written order
        \\and costs `8` bytes more for it. `Packed` keeps the written order and costs
        \\nothing, at the price of a `u64` sitting at offset `1`. Neither directive
        \\exists on a union: `union #packed { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'packed'`, since a union's size is fixed by its tag
        \\and its largest payload.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Header :: struct #packed {
        \\    magic:   u32;
        \\    version: u8;
        \\    length:  u32;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Inner  :: struct { a: u8; b: u64; }
        \\Nested :: struct #packed { tag: u8; inner: Inner; }
        \\
        \\offset :: (base: *$T, field: *$U) -> u64 {
        \\    return cast(u64)field - cast(u64)base;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    h: Header;
        \\    print("Header size=% magic=% version=% length=%\n", size_of(Header),
        \\          offset(*h, *h.magic), offset(*h, *h.version), offset(*h, *h.length));
        \\    h.magic = 1; h.version = 2; h.length = 3;
        \\    print("read back: % % %\n", h.magic, h.version, h.length);
        \\    print("Inner=% Nested=%\n", size_of(Inner), size_of(Nested));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\Header size=9 magic=0 version=4 length=5
        \\read back: 1 2 3
        \\Inner=16 Nested=17
    );
    note(30, #string,oneline
        \\`#packed` is not contagious. It removes the padding this record would have
        \\inserted between its own fields; a field whose type is another aggregate
        \\keeps that aggregate's own size, padding included. `Nested` is `1 + 16`,
        \\not `1 + 9`. A wire format made of nested records needs `#packed` on every
        \\level.
    );
    subheading(31, "Reflection reports declaration order");
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Plain :: struct { a: u8; b: u64; c: u8; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("fields: % % %\n",
        \\          field_name(Plain, 0), field_name(Plain, 1), field_name(Plain, 2));
        \\    print("count=%\n", field_count(Plain));
        \\    print("types: % %\n",
        \\          type_name(field_type(Plain, 0)), type_name(field_type(Plain, 1)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\fields: a b c
        \\count=3
        \\types: u8 u64
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\`field_count`, `field_name`, and `field_type` walk the storage fields in
        \\declaration order. The sorted order is used only to compute sizes and to
        \\emit code, and is not exposed. The index argument must be a compile-time
        \\constant: neither `for i: 0..field_count(T)` nor `#inline for` over the same
        \\range works, and both are `error: unknown const i`. A traversal is written
        \\as a recursive procedure whose index is a parameter, evaluated by
        \\`#comptime`.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Plain :: struct { a: u8; b: u64; c: u8; }
        \\
        \\names :: (T: type, i: s64) -> string {
        \\    if i >= field_count(T) return "";
        \\    return str_cat(str_cat(field_name(T, i), " "), names(T, i + 1));
        \\}
        \\
        \\PLAIN_FIELDS :: #comptime names(Plain, 0);
        \\
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", PLAIN_FIELDS); }
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\a b c
    );
}

render_section_39 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Three directives may decorate a struct field. They look alike and are often
        \\confused; they do three unrelated things, and one of them currently does
        \\nothing at all.
    );
    table_header(1, "Marker   —   Written   —   Effect on the struct");
    table_row(2, "`using`   |   `using base: Base;`   |   None. Parsed and discarded.");
    table_row(3, "`#empty`   |   `#empty: $T;`   |   Declares a field with no storage.");
    table_row(4, "`#as`   |   `#as value: s64;`   |   Marks the implicit conversion field.");
    subheading(5, "`using` on a field promotes nothing");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\A `using` field is an ordinary field. The parser consumes the keyword and
        \\records nothing about it, so the struct has the same fields, the same size,
        \\and the same member namespace it would have had without it. There is no
        \\member promotion and no inheritance.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Base    :: struct { x: s64 = 1; y: s64 = 2; }
        \\Derived :: struct { using base: Base; z: s64 = 3; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    d: Derived;
        \\    print("through the field: % % %\n", d.base.x, d.base.y, d.z);
        \\    print("size=% field_count=% names=% %\n", size_of(Derived),
        \\          field_count(Derived), field_name(Derived, 0), field_name(Derived, 1));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\through the field: 1 2 3
        \\size=24 field_count=2 names=base z
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Reaching a base field by its bare name fails: `d.x` is
        \\`error: no field 'x' in 'Derived'` with `help: did you mean 'z'?`, and the
        \\suggestion naming a sibling rather than a base member is the clearest sign
        \\that the marker had no effect. `d.base.x` is the spelling that works.
    );
    subheading(10, "`using` as a statement and as a parameter modifier");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The two forms that do work are both about a binding rather than about a
        \\type. `using b;` opens a struct-typed local so its fields are readable as
        \\bare names; `(using b: Base)` on a parameter is rewritten by the parser into
        \\exactly that statement at the top of the body.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Base :: struct { x: s64 = 1; y: s64 = 2; }
        \\
        \\sum :: (using b: Base) -> s64 { return x + y; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("parameter: %\n", sum(Base.{ 4, 5 }));
        \\
        \\    local := Base.{ 10, 20 };
        \\    using local;
        \\    print("promoted:  % %\n", x, y);
        \\    local.x = 99;
        \\    print("re-read:   %\n", x);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\parameter: 9
        \\promoted:  10 20
        \\re-read:   99
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The promoted names read the original storage, not a snapshot: writing
        \\`local.x` afterwards changes what the bare `x` reports. The promotion is
        \\block-scoped and ends with the block that opened it, so a `using` inside a
        \\nested block leaves nothing behind: reading the promoted name after that
        \\block is `error: unbound x`.
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\Promoted names are read-only. `x = 5;` after `using local;` is
        \\`error: unbound 'x'`, because the promotion lives in expression resolution
        \\and never installs an assignable binding. Write through the original name.
    );
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\The operand must be a struct value. A pointer is
        \\`error: using expects a struct, got *Base`, and there is no automatic
        \\dereference. It must also be a plain name, not a path: `using d.base;` stops
        \\at the identifier and the remaining `.base` is read as a variant shorthand,
        \\giving `error: enum shorthand needs an enum context`.
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\A promoted name is the last thing an identifier lookup consults. A local, a
        \\global, or a constant of the same name wins, so opening a struct that
        \\happens to have an `x` cannot capture an existing `x`. Among several open
        \\`using` statements the earliest one wins, not the most recent, because the
        \\open list is searched in the order the statements were executed.
    );
    subheading(18, "`#empty`: fields with no storage");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`#empty: T;` declares a marker field. It occupies no bytes, has no name a
        \\program can write, and is filtered out of the storage fields, so it is
        \\invisible to `size_of`, `field_count`, and every literal form. Several
        \\markers may share one directive, separated by commas and terminated by a
        \\semicolon; omitting the terminator is
        \\`error: missing ';' after #empty field marker`.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Phantom :: struct { #empty: $T; raw: s64; }
        \\Marker  :: struct { #empty: u8, u16; v: s64 = 9; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Phantom(f64) = .{ .raw = 5 };
        \\    q: Phantom(s8)  = .{ .raw = 6 };
        \\    m: Marker;
        \\    print("phantom size=% fields=% raw=% name=%\n", size_of(type_of(p)),
        \\          field_count(type_of(p)), p.raw, type_name(type_of(p)));
        \\    print("other instance name=%\n", type_name(type_of(q)));
        \\    print("marker size=% fields=% first=% v=%\n", size_of(Marker),
        \\          field_count(Marker), field_name(Marker, 0), m.v);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\phantom size=8 fields=1 raw=5 name=Phantom_f64
        \\other instance name=Phantom_s8
        \\marker size=8 fields=1 first=v v=9
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\The generic form is the one that earns its keep. Type-parameter collection
        \\still walks the annotation of an `#empty` field, so `#empty: $T;` makes the
        \\struct generic over a type it does not store. `Phantom(f64)` and
        \\`Phantom(s8)` are then distinct types of eight bytes each, and assigning one
        \\to the other is `error: got Phantom_f64, expected Phantom_s8`. The standard
        \\library uses this for containers that keep their elements behind an
        \\indirection, such as `LinkedList` in `std/Container/LinkedList.glo` and
        \\`RangeFull` in the prelude.
    );
    note(23, #string,oneline
        \\`#empty:` in a parameter list is a different feature with the same
        \\spelling. `f :: (#empty: $T, ...)` declares a type-witness parameter that
        \\carries a type and no value; it is described in the chapter on compile-time
        \\and type-only parameters. Nothing about the field form applies to it.
    );
    subheading(24, "`#as`: the conversion field");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\`#as` marks one stored field as the struct's implicit conversion target. A
        \\value of the struct is then accepted anywhere that field's type is expected,
        \\and the compiler substitutes the field. This is how a wrapper stays a
        \\distinct type while remaining usable as the thing it wraps.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Id :: struct { tag: u8; #as value: s64; }
        \\
        \\double :: (v: s64) -> s64 { return v * 2; }
        \\give   :: (i: Id) -> s64 { return i; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i: Id = .{ .tag = 3, .value = 77 };
        \\    n: s64 = i;
        \\    m: s64;
        \\    m = i;
        \\    print("declaration: %\n", n);
        \\    print("assignment:  %\n", m);
        \\    print("argument:    %\n", double(i));
        \\    print("return:      %\n", give(i));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\declaration: 77
        \\assignment:  77
        \\argument:    154
        \\return:      77
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\The conversion is outward only. There is no route from the field's type back
        \\into the struct: with `f :: (i: Id)`, the call `f(5)` is
        \\`error: arg type int != Id in call f`. The struct is still a distinct type;
        \\`#as` only widens where it may be spent.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\It fires only where a concrete expected type already exists, which is the
        \\same set of positions the inferred literal `.{ ... }` works in. Operator
        \\resolution is not one of them: with the `Id` above, `i + 1` is
        \\`error: no operator '+' for Id, int`. Operators are chosen by the operand
        \\types, before any coercion is attempted.
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The conversion is one step deep. If `Outer` has `#as i: Inner` and `Inner`
        \\has `#as v: s64`, then `n: s64 = o;` is `error: got Outer, expected int`;
        \\the compiler tests the marked field's type against the expectation and does
        \\not recur.
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\Two further details. Writing `#as` on more than one field is accepted and
        \\the last marker wins, since the record keeps a single field name. `#as` and
        \\`using` combine on one field in either order, `#as using inner: Base;` or
        \\`using #as inner: Base;`, though the `using` half still does nothing. `#as`
        \\is a struct-only directive: on a union variant it is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'as'`.
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\`Path` in `std/File_System/Path.glo` is the standard library's use of it.
        \\The struct carries a style and an allocator alongside the text, and marks
        \\the text as the conversion field, so a `Path` can be handed to any procedure
        \\that takes a `string`.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "File_System";
        \\
        \\length_of :: (s: string) -> s64 { return s.count; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Path = .{ .value = "src/main.glo" };
        \\    print("as string: % (%)\n", length_of(p), p.value);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\as string: 12 (src/main.glo)
    );
}

render_section_40 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A `struct`, `union`, `enum`, or `enum_flags` body may be written directly
        \\where a field's type annotation goes. The aggregate is not anonymous: the
        \\compiler gives it a nominal name built from the field it was written on,
        \\hoists the declaration out to the enclosing scope, and puts that name in the
        \\annotation. This is the only nesting a struct body supports; a named
        \\`Inner :: struct { ... }` inside a struct body is
        \\`error: unexpected token '::'`.
    );
    table_header(1, "Written as a field type   —   Declares");
    table_row(2, "`struct { ... }`   |   A record, with the full struct directive set.");
    table_row(3, "`union { ... }`   |   A tagged union; `union #raw { ... }` for an untagged one.");
    table_row(4, "`enum { ... }` or `enum u8 { ... }`   |   An enum, with an optional backing type.");
    table_row(5, "`enum_flags u8 { ... }`   |   A flag enum; the backing type stays mandatory.");
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Outer :: struct {
        \\    inner: struct { a: s64; b: s64; };
        \\    kind:  enum { One; Two; };
        \\    perm:  enum_flags u8 { Read :: 1; Write :: 2; };
        \\    tag:   union { L: s64; R: bool; };
        \\}
        \\
        \\sum :: (v: Outer.inner) -> s64 { return v.a + v.b; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    o: Outer;
        \\    o.inner = .{ 1, 2 };
        \\    o.kind  = .Two;
        \\    o.perm  = .Write;
        \\    o.tag.L = 5;
        \\    print("values: % % % %\n", o.inner.a, o.inner.b, o.kind, o.perm);
        \\    print("inner name: %\n", type_name(type_of(o.inner)));
        \\    print("kind name:  %\n", type_name(type_of(o.kind)));
        \\    print("perm name:  %\n", type_name(type_of(o.perm)));
        \\    print("tag name:   %\n", type_name(type_of(o.tag)));
        \\    if o.tag == {
        \\        case .L(v); print("tag L %\n", v);
        \\        case .R(b); print("tag R %\n", b);
        \\    }
        \\    standalone: Outer.inner = .{ 3, 4 };
        \\    print("named annotation: % %\n", sum(o.inner), sum(standalone));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\values: 1 2 1 2
        \\inner name: Outer.inner
        \\kind name:  Outer.kind
        \\perm name:  Outer.perm
        \\tag name:   Outer.tag
        \\tag L 5
        \\named annotation: 3 7
    );
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\The name is `Outer.field`: the owning aggregate, a dot, and the field name.
        \\`type_name` reports it verbatim, and it is a real type name, so it may be
        \\written in any annotation, including a parameter, a return type, and a
        \\declaration of a value that is not stored in an `Outer` at all. The inline
        \\form is therefore a placement convenience, not a scoping mechanism: the type
        \\is as visible as the outer declaration is.
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The dotted spelling is annotation syntax only. In expression position the
        \\dot is member access, so `size_of(Outer.inner)` is
        \\`error: member of non-struct`. Where a type value is needed, take it from a
        \\binding with `type_of`.
    );
    subheading(10, "Nesting and composition");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The name hint follows the field being parsed, so an inline aggregate may
        \\appear anywhere inside that field's type: under a pointer, under an array or
        \\view, under an option, and inside a further inline aggregate. A union
        \\variant's payload is an aggregate field too, so the same spelling works
        \\there and yields `Union.Variant`.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Outer :: struct {
        \\    inner: struct {
        \\        deep: struct { n: s64; };
        \\        m: s64;
        \\    };
        \\    items: []struct { v: s64; };
        \\    slot:  ?*struct { w: s64; };
        \\}
        \\
        \\Msg :: union {
        \\    Header: struct { id: s64; len: s64; };
        \\    Eof:    void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    o: Outer;
        \\    o.inner.deep.n = 3;
        \\    print("deep=% name=%\n", o.inner.deep.n, type_name(type_of(o.inner.deep)));
        \\    print("items elem name=%\n", type_name(type_of(o.items)));
        \\    print("slot name=%\n", type_name(type_of(o.slot)));
        \\    m := Msg.Header(.{ .id = 1, .len = 2 });
        \\    if m == {
        \\        case .Header(h); print("header % %  name=%\n", h.id, h.len,
        \\                               type_name(type_of(h)));
        \\        case .Eof;       print("eof\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\deep=3 name=Outer.inner.deep
        \\items elem name=glo_Arr_Outer.items
        \\slot name=Option__Outer_slot
        \\header 1 2  name=Msg.Header
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\A nested inline aggregate composes its name from the path it was reached
        \\by, giving `Outer.inner.deep`. Under a type constructor the element name is
        \\still `Outer.items`, and the constructor's own mangled name wraps it, which
        \\is why the view prints as `glo_Arr_Outer.items` and the option as
        \\`Option__Outer_slot`.
    );
    note(15, #string,oneline
        \\One inline aggregate per field. The name is derived from the field alone, so
        \\a field whose type mentions two of them collides with itself:
        \\`both: (struct { p: s64; }, struct { q: s64; });` is
        \\`error: type 'Outer.both' is already declared`. Give at least one of them a
        \\name of its own.
    );
    subheading(16, "Directives on an inline aggregate");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\An inline body is parsed by the same procedure as a named one, so it takes
        \\the same directives. A field may be a packed record, a C-layout record, a
        \\derived record, a narrow enum, or a raw union, without any of them needing a
        \\top-level declaration.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Outer :: struct {
        \\    head:  struct #packed { a: u8; b: u64; };
        \\    body:  struct #c_call { a: u8; b: u64; };
        \\    shown: struct #derive(Show) { n: s64; };
        \\    small: enum u8 { A; B; };
        \\    bits:  union #raw { i: s64; f: f64; };
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    o: Outer;
        \\    o.shown.n = 4;
        \\    o.small = .B;
        \\    o.bits.f = 1.0;
        \\    print("head=% body=% small=% bits=%\n", size_of(type_of(o.head)),
        \\          size_of(type_of(o.body)), size_of(type_of(o.small)),
        \\          size_of(type_of(o.bits)));
        \\    print("shown=%\n", o.shown);
        \\    print("raw read: %\n", o.bits.i);
        \\    print("Outer=%\n", size_of(Outer));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\head=9 body=16 small=1 bits=8
        \\shown={4}
        \\raw read: 4607182418800017408
        \\Outer=48
    );
    subheading(20, "Where an inline aggregate is refused");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\Everywhere else. A type body written outside an aggregate field is
        \\`error: inline aggregate types are only allowed in aggregate fields`, with
        \\the span on the `struct`, `union`, or `enum` keyword.
    );
    table_header(22, "Position   —   Accepted");
    table_row(23, "Struct field type   |   Yes, including under `*`, `[]`, `[N]`, `?`, and tuples.");
    table_row(24, "Union variant payload   |   Yes, named `Union.Variant`.");
    table_row(25, "Local declaration   |   No.");
    table_row(26, "Parameter or return type   |   No.");
    table_row(27, "`cast` target   |   No.");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\The rule is a naming rule rather than a policy. The parser can only mint a
        \\nominal name when it already knows an owner and a field, and it knows both
        \\only while it is parsing a struct field or a union variant. Elsewhere there
        \\is nothing to call the type, so it is rejected rather than made anonymous.
        \\A type that is needed in a signature is declared at the top level and named
        \\once.
    );
}

render_section_41 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A tuple is an anonymous product type written `(A, B, C)`. It has no
        \\declaration and no name of its own: its identity is its element list,
        \\so two procedures that independently write `(s64, string)` agree on
        \\the type. The elements are positional and unnamed, which is the whole
        \\difference between a tuple and a `struct`.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The literal is `.(a, b, c)` and element access is `.0`, `.1`, and so
        \\on. The index is a decimal literal that the parser reads as part of
        \\the access, not an expression, so a named constant does not work
        \\there: `t.i` is `error: tuple member must be a numeric field like .0`.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: (s64, string, bool) = .(7, "hi", true);
        \\    print("% % %\n", t.0, t.1, t.2);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\7 hi 1
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\An annotation supplies the element types and the element count. With
        \\no annotation the element types are inferred from the values, and an
        \\integer literal stays a compile-time number: `.(5)` has the element
        \\type `comptime_int`. A literal whose length disagrees with the
        \\annotation is `error: tuple literal has 2 value(s), expected 3`.
    );
    table_header(5, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(6, "`(A, B, C)`   |   The tuple type. Element order is significant and there are no names.");
    table_row(7, "`.(a, b, c)`   |   The tuple literal. Its length must match the expected type exactly.");
    table_row(8, "`t.0`, `t.1`   |   Element access. The index is a literal, never an expression.");
    table_row(9, "`a, b := expr`   |   Destructuring declaration, one name per element.");
    table_row(10, "`.()`   |   The empty tuple, whose type is `void`.");
    subheading(11, "Returning more than one value");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\There is no separate multiple-return mechanism. A procedure that
        \\produces several values declares a tuple return type and returns a
        \\tuple literal; the caller destructures it in a declaration.
    );
    code_block(13, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\divmod :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> (s64, s64) {
        \\    return .(a / b, a % b);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    q, r := divmod(17, 5);
        \\    print("% remainder %\n", q, r);
        \\    _, only := divmod(9, 2);
        \\    print("%\n", only);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\3 remainder 2
        \\1
    );
    bullet(15, #string,oneline
        \\Destructuring reads any tuple value, not only a call result. With
        \\`t: (s64, string)` in scope, `a, b := t;` binds both elements.
    );
    bullet(16, #string,oneline
        \\`_` in a destructuring position discards that element.
    );
    bullet(17, #string,oneline
        \\Destructuring is a declaration form only. Assigning into existing
        \\variables, `x, y = divmod(9, 2);`, is `error: unexpected token '='`.
    );
    bullet(18, #string,oneline
        \\Binding the wrong number of names is
        \\`error: destructuring binds 3 names from a 2-value result`, and an
        \\index past the end is
        \\`error: tuple index .2 is out of range for 2-value tuple`.
    );
    subheading(19, "Tuples are not field-reordered");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\An undecorated `struct` is laid out by sorting its fields by
        \\descending alignment, so a record can be smaller than the sum of its
        \\declared members. A tuple keeps declaration order, because the
        \\program names element positions directly with `.0` and `.1` and a
        \\reordering would silently renumber them.
    );
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Record :: struct { a: u8; b: s64; c: u8; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: (u8, s64, u8);
        \\    print("struct % tuple %\n", size_of(Record), size_of(type_of(t)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\struct 16 tuple 24
    );
    note(23, #string,oneline
        \\The same three members cost 16 bytes as a struct and 24 as a tuple.
        \\A tuple is the right shape for a short-lived group of values, such as
        \\a multiple return; a type that will be stored in quantity should be a
        \\struct. Layout is covered in the chapter on struct memory layout.
    );
    subheading(24, "`void` is the empty tuple");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\`.()` is the zero-element tuple literal and its type is `void`, the
        \\same `void` a procedure with no return annotation produces and the
        \\same `void` that spells a payload-less union variant. It occupies no
        \\storage.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    e := .();
        \\    print("% %\n", type_name(type_of(e)), size_of(type_of(e)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\void 0
    );
    bullet(28, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` element inside a larger tuple is rejected:
        \\`error: a zero-sized 'void' member inside a struct, tuple, or array is not supported yet; remove the member, or wrap it in a single-field struct`.
        \\Erasing it would renumber the elements after it.
    );
    subheading(29, "Current limits");
    bullet(30, #string,oneline
        \\Tuples have no `Show` instance. Passing one to `print` is rejected
        \\with error: variadic arg type `tuple` does not implement `Show` in
        \\call `print`. Print the elements individually.
    );
    bullet(31, #string,oneline
        \\An element cannot be assigned. On a `(s64, s64)` value, `t.0 = 10;`
        \\reaches lowering and fails with
        \\`error: internal error: glo_tup_long_long has no storage field '0'`.
        \\Build a replacement tuple instead.
    );
    bullet(32, #string,oneline
        \\`n.0.0` does not lex: `0.0` is a float literal, and the compiler
        \\reports `error: unexpected token '0.0'`. Bind the inner tuple to a
        \\name first, then index it.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\`(T)` in type position is the parenthesized type `T`, not a
        \\one-element tuple: `one: (s64) = .(5);` is
        \\`error: got tuple, expected int`.
    );
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\There is no tuple pattern. `#pattern .(a, b) = t` is
        \\`error: unexpected token '('`; destructure with `a, b := t;`.
    );
}

render_section_42 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An enum is a named set of integer constants that share a type of
        \\their own. Each member is written on its own line and, like every
        \\other aggregate member, ends with a semicolon; a comma is
        \\`error: enum member 'Red' must end with ';'`.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\An explicit value is attached with `::`, never with `=`. `=` is the
        \\assignment and field-default spelling everywhere else in the
        \\language, while `::` is how a constant is bound, and an enum member
        \\is a constant. Writing `=` is rejected outright with
        \\`error: enum variant integer values use '::', not '='` rather than
        \\being quietly accepted as a second spelling.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green :: 5; Blue; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % %\n", cast(s64)Color.Red, cast(s64)Color.Green, cast(s64)Color.Blue);
        \\    print("size % count %\n", size_of(Color), enum_count(Color));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\0 5 6
        \\size 8 count 3
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Numbering starts at zero and each member takes one more than the
        \\member before it. An explicit value resets that counter, so `Blue`
        \\above is 6 and not 2. The value after `::` is a single integer literal
        \\token, not an expression: naming a constant, `Red :: K;`, is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'K'`, and a negative literal, `Low :: -1;`,
        \\is `error: unexpected token '-'`. Hexadecimal literals such as `16#1`
        \\are accepted, and two members may carry the same value.
    );
    subheading(5, "Backing type and size");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\The backing type is written between `enum` and the brace. It fixes
        \\the size and the alignment of the type; with no backing type an enum
        \\is 8 bytes.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Byte_Code :: enum u8  { Push; Pop; Halt; }
        \\Wide_Code :: enum s32 { Push; Pop; Halt; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("u8 % s32 %\n", size_of(Byte_Code), size_of(Wide_Code));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\u8 1 s32 4
    );
    table_header(9, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(10, "`Name :: enum { A; B; }`   |   Default backing type, 8 bytes.");
    table_row(11, "`Name :: enum u8 { A; B; }`   |   Size and alignment follow the named backing type.");
    table_row(12, "`Name :: enum_flags u32 { ... }`   |   Flag enum. The backing type is mandatory here.");
    table_row(13, "`A :: 5;` in the body   |   Explicit value; the next member continues from 6.");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\Neither the declared value nor a conversion is range-checked against
        \\the backing type. `enum u8 { Red :: 300; }` compiles and `Red` reads
        \\back as 44, and `cast(Color)9` produces 9 on a three-member enum. An
        \\enum is a typed integer, not a proof that the integer is one of the
        \\declared members.
    );
    subheading(15, "Naming a member");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\The qualified form `Color.Blue` works anywhere. The dotted shorthand
        \\`.Blue` works wherever an enum type is already expected: an annotated
        \\declaration, an argument position, the other side of a comparison, a
        \\`return`, and a `case` label.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; }
        \\
        \\pick :: (c: Color) -> Color { if c == .Red return .Blue; return .Red; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Color = .Green;
        \\    print("annotation: %\n", cast(s64)c);
        \\    print("argument: %\n", cast(s64)pick(.Red));
        \\    print("comparison: %\n", c == .Green);
        \\    if c == {
        \\        case .Green; print("case: green\n");
        \\        case .Red;   print("case: red\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\annotation: 1
        \\argument: 2
        \\comparison: 1
        \\case: green
    );
    bullet(19, #string,oneline
        \\A shorthand with no expected type is
        \\`error: enum shorthand needs an enum context`, and an unknown member
        \\is `error: no enum variant Color.Purple`.
    );
    bullet(20, #string,oneline
        \\A plain `if x == { ... }` switch over an enum is not
        \\exhaustiveness-checked; the example above compiles with `Blue`
        \\uncovered. `if #pattern x == { ... }` is checked, and reports
        \\`error: non-exhaustive #pattern switch` with
        \\`note: missing case: Color.Blue`. The chapter on exhaustiveness,
        \\guards, and reachability has the whole rule.
    );
    subheading(21, "Reflection");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`enum_count(T)` is the number of members; `enum_name(T, i)` and
        \\`enum_value(T, i)` report the member at declaration index `i`. The
        \\index has to be a compile-time constant, so an ordinary `for i:
        \\0..enum_count(T)` loop over them is `error: unknown const i`.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green :: 5; Blue; }
        \\
        \\name_of :: (c: Color) -> string {
        \\    if c == {
        \\        case .Red;   return "Red";
        \\        case .Green; return "Green";
        \\        case .Blue;  return "Blue";
        \\    }
        \\    return "?";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Color = .Blue;
        \\    print("% is %\n", name_of(c), cast(s64)c);
        \\    print("% raw prints as %\n", enum_name(Color, 2), c);
        \\    print("declared: % = %, % = %, % = %\n",
        \\          enum_name(Color, 0), enum_value(Color, 0),
        \\          enum_name(Color, 1), enum_value(Color, 1),
        \\          enum_name(Color, 2), enum_value(Color, 2));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\Blue is 6
        \\Blue raw prints as 6
        \\declared: Red = 0, Green = 5, Blue = 6
    );
    note(25, #string,oneline
        \\The `Show` instance for enums is the integer one, so `print("%", c)`
        \\prints the numeric value and never the member name. Printing a name
        \\means either a switch, as in `name_of` above, or `enum_name` with a
        \\literal index.
    );
    subheading(26, "Flag enums");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`enum_flags` declares an enum whose members are intended to be
        \\combined bitwise. The backing type is not optional here:
        \\`enum_flags { Read; }` is `error: unexpected token '{'`, because the
        \\parser reads a type immediately after the keyword.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`enum_flags` assigns no bit patterns. Members are numbered
        \\sequentially exactly as in a plain enum, and the marker is recorded on
        \\the declaration and then never read again by the checker: it documents
        \\intent and nothing else. A flag set written without explicit values is
        \\therefore wrong in a way that still compiles and still runs.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Perm :: enum_flags u32 { Read; Write; Exec; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("Read=% Write=% Exec=%\n", cast(u32)Perm.Read, cast(u32)Perm.Write, cast(u32)Perm.Exec);
        \\    p := Perm.Read | Perm.Exec;
        \\    print("Read|Exec raw=% holds Write=%\n", cast(u32)p, (p & Perm.Write) == Perm.Write);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\Read=0 Write=1 Exec=2
        \\Read|Exec raw=2 holds Write=0
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\`Read` is 0, so `Read | Exec` is `Exec` alone, and the set claims not
        \\to hold `Write` only because `Write` happens to be 1 and `Exec` is 2.
        \\Every member of a flag enum must carry an explicit power-of-two value.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Perm :: enum_flags u32 {
        \\    None  :: 0;
        \\    Read  :: 16#1;
        \\    Write :: 16#2;
        \\    Exec  :: 16#4;
        \\}
        \\
        \\holds :: (set: Perm, flag: Perm) -> bool { return (set & flag) == flag; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p := Perm.Read | Perm.Exec;
        \\    print("raw=%\n", cast(u32)p);
        \\    print("read=% write=% exec=%\n", holds(p, Perm.Read), holds(p, Perm.Write), holds(p, Perm.Exec));
        \\    p = p | Perm.Write;
        \\    print("after add write: raw=%\n", cast(u32)p);
        \\    p = p ^ Perm.Exec;
        \\    print("after clear exec: raw=%\n", cast(u32)p);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\raw=5
        \\read=1 write=0 exec=1
        \\after add write: raw=7
        \\after clear exec: raw=3
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\Four operators are defined directly on enum operands by the compiler
        \\rather than through a typeclass instance. The three binary ones take
        \\two values of the same enum type; all four produce that enum type
        \\again, so a combination stays typed and does not decay to an integer.
    );
    table_header(35, "Expression   —   Result");
    table_row(36, "`a | b`   |   Set union: the bits of both operands.");
    table_row(37, "`a & b`   |   Set intersection, the usual membership test when compared back to `b`.");
    table_row(38, "`a ^ b`   |   Symmetric difference; clears a flag that is known to be present.");
    table_row(39, "`~a`   |   Complement over the backing type's width.");
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\Both operands must be the same enum type. `Perm.Read | 4` is
        \\`error: no operator '|' for Perm, int`; convert the enum with `cast`
        \\if raw bits are wanted.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\Arithmetic is not defined: `Perm.Read + Perm.Write` is
        \\`error: no operator '+' for Perm, Perm`.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\The compound forms are not defined either. `p |= Perm.Write;` is
        \\`error: no operator '|=' for Perm, Perm`; write `p = p | Perm.Write;`.
    );
    bullet(43, #string,oneline
        \\Because the operators are attached to the enum kind and not to
        \\`enum_flags`, they work on a plain `enum` as well. `enum_flags` is
        \\the signal to a reader that combination is intended.
    );
}

render_section_43 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A `union` holds exactly one of several named variants at a time,
        \\together with a tag that records which one. Each variant is written
        \\`Name: Type;` and, like a struct member, ends with a semicolon; a
        \\comma is `error: union member 'A' must end with ';'`. A variant that
        \\carries nothing is declared with the payload type `void`.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Vec :: struct { x: s64; y: s64; }
        \\
        \\Shape :: union {
        \\    Circle: s64;
        \\    Box:    Vec;
        \\    Empty:  void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\area :: (s: Shape) -> s64 {
        \\    if s == {
        \\        case .Circle(r); return 3 * r * r;
        \\        case .Box(q);    return q.x * q.y;
        \\        case .Empty;     return 0;
        \\    }
        \\    return -1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s := Shape.Circle(5);
        \\    print("% %\n", area(s), size_of(Shape));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\75 24
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Variant names are capitalised by convention and nothing enforces it.
        \\A payload is never read by writing `s.Circle`; the only way in is a
        \\pattern capture, which the chapter on reading a union payload covers
        \\in full. Unlike an enum, a union subject makes a plain
        \\`if x == { ... }` switch exhaustiveness-checked, so a missing arm is
        \\`error: switch is exhaustive by default; add else or #partial`; the
        \\chapter on exhaustiveness, guards, and reachability has the details.
    );
    subheading(4, "The tag, the size, and the alignment");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\A tagged union is laid out as an 8-byte tag followed by the largest
        \\payload, and the record takes the larger of the tag's alignment and
        \\the largest payload alignment. `void` variants contribute nothing to
        \\either figure. The tag is 8 bytes whatever the variant count, so the
        \\two-variant `union { A: u8; B: u8; }` is 16 bytes.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Tiny :: union { A: u8;  B: u8; }
        \\Big  :: union { A: [24]u8; B: u8; }
        \\Wide :: union { A: Simd(f32, 4); B: u8; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("Tiny % / %\n", size_of(Tiny), type_info(Tiny).alignment);
        \\    print("Big  % / %\n", size_of(Big),  type_info(Big).alignment);
        \\    print("Wide % / %\n", size_of(Wide), type_info(Wide).alignment);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\Tiny 16 / 8
        \\Big  32 / 8
        \\Wide 32 / 16
    );
    bullet(8, #string,oneline
        \\`Wide` shows the alignment rule: a 16-byte SIMD payload raises the
        \\record's alignment to 16, so the payload starts at offset 16 and the
        \\whole union is 32 bytes rather than 24.
    );
    bullet(9, #string,oneline
        \\A union declared in a program never gets a niche. `union { A: void; B: *u8; }`
        \\is 16 bytes even though the pointer has a spare null representation.
        \\Only the compiler's own `?T` and `E!T` are laid out that way; see the
        \\chapters on the option and result types.
    );
    bullet(10, #string,oneline
        \\There is no layout control. `union #packed { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'packed'` and `union #c_call { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'c_call'`. A union that must match a C
        \\layout is a raw union, covered in its own chapter.
    );
    bullet(11, #string,oneline
        \\A default-initialized union, `s: Shape;`, holds the first declared
        \\variant with a zeroed payload.
    );
    subheading(12, "Constructing a value");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\Six forms build a union value. Two name the union explicitly, two go
        \\through a variable that already carries the type, and two rely on an
        \\expected type to supply it.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\tag_of :: (s: Shape) -> string {
        \\    if s == { case .Circle; return "Circle"; case .Empty; return "Empty"; }
        \\    return "?";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := Shape.Circle(1);          // qualified call
        \\    b: Shape = .Circle(2);         // dotted call, type from the annotation
        \\    c: Shape;  c.Circle = 3;       // variant assignment
        \\    d: Shape;  d.Empty;            // bare variant statement
        \\    e := Shape.Empty();            // qualified void variant, parentheses required
        \\    f: Shape = .Empty;             // dotted void variant, no parentheses
        \\    print("% % % % % %\n", tag_of(a), tag_of(b), tag_of(c), tag_of(d), tag_of(e), tag_of(f));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\Circle Circle Circle Empty Empty Empty
    );
    table_header(16, "Form   —   What it does");
    table_row(17, "`Shape.Circle(1)`   |   Qualified call. Works anywhere; needs no expected type.");
    table_row(18, "`.Circle(2)`   |   Dotted call. The expected type supplies the union.");
    table_row(19, "`c.Circle = 3;`   |   Selects the variant and stores the payload in one step.");
    table_row(20, "`d.Empty;`   |   Bare statement. Stores the tag of a `void` variant.");
    table_row(21, "`Shape.Empty()`   |   Qualified `void` variant. The empty parentheses are required.");
    table_row(22, "`f: Shape = .Empty;`   |   Dotted `void` variant. No parentheses.");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\The parentheses rule falls out of what the qualified form is. `Shape`
        \\is a type, and `Shape.Circle` asks for a member of it, which a type
        \\does not have; only the call `Shape.Circle(1)` is recognised as
        \\construction. So `x := Shape.Empty;` is
        \\`error: member of non-struct`, and so is `x := Shape.Circle;`. The
        \\dotted form is not a call at all, which is why `.Empty` stands alone.
        \\The same applies to the built-in option: write `(?s64).None()`, not
        \\`(?s64).None`.
    );
    bullet(24, #string,oneline
        \\A dotted variant with no expected type has nothing to resolve
        \\against: `x := .Empty;` is
        \\`error: enum shorthand needs an enum context`.
    );
    bullet(25, #string,oneline
        \\An unknown variant is `error: 'Q' is not a variant of union S` in the
        \\dotted call form and `error: no union field Q` in the assignment
        \\form.
    );
    bullet(26, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` variant has no payload to assign: `s.Empty = 1;` is
        \\`error: assign int to void`. Use the bare statement `s.Empty;`.
    );
    subheading(27, "Generic unions");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A `$T` written in a variant's payload type introduces a type
        \\parameter, exactly as it does in a struct. The union is then applied
        \\like any other generic type, and it may refer to itself through a
        \\pointer.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Tree :: union {
        \\    Leaf: $T;
        \\    Pair: *Tree(T);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Tree(s64) = .Leaf(4);
        \\    n := 0;
        \\    if t == { case .Leaf(v); n = v; case .Pair(p); n = -1; }
        \\    print("% %\n", type_name(type_of(t)), n);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\Tree_int 4
    );
    bullet(31, #string,oneline
        \\A parameter is introduced once with `$` and spelled as plain `T`
        \\everywhere after that, including in the union's own name. The
        \\instantiated type is named by mangling the arguments onto the
        \\declaration name, which is why `Tree(s64)` reports as `Tree_int`.
    );
    subheading(32, "Variant reflection");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\Three compile-time queries describe a union's variants in declaration
        \\order.
    );
    table_header(34, "Call   —   Result");
    table_row(35, "`variant_count(T)`   |   Number of variants, as an `s64`.");
    table_row(36, "`variant_name(T, i)`   |   The variant's name, as a `string`.");
    table_row(37, "`variant_type(T, i)`   |   The payload type; `void` for a payload-less variant.");
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Vec :: struct { x: s64; y: s64; }
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Box: Vec; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("count %\n", variant_count(Shape));
        \\    print("0: % %\n", variant_name(Shape, 0), type_name(variant_type(Shape, 0)));
        \\    print("1: % %\n", variant_name(Shape, 1), type_name(variant_type(Shape, 1)));
        \\    print("2: % %\n", variant_name(Shape, 2), type_name(variant_type(Shape, 2)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\count 3
        \\0: Circle int
        \\1: Box Vec
        \\2: Empty void
    );
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\The index must be a compile-time constant, so a runtime `for` over
        \\`variant_count` is `error: unknown const i`. An index past the last
        \\variant is `error: variant index 5 is out of range`.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\Three directives may follow the `union` keyword: `#raw`, `#derive(...)`,
        \\and the compiler-internal `#magic`. A single variant may additionally
        \\carry `#from`. Each has its own chapter.
    );
}

render_section_44 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A tagged union's payload is only readable through a pattern match.
        \\Writing `s.Circle` as an expression is a compile error even when the
        \\preceding line stored that very variant.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Box: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s := Shape.Circle(5);
        \\    print("%\n", s.Circle);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\error: tagged-union payload '.Circle' may only be bound by a pattern match
        \\note: match on the union and use that arm's payload capture, or enable #enable(allow_union_unwrap) for unsafe access
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The tag is the only evidence that a payload is the one being read,
        \\and a member access has nowhere to put the test that consults it. A
        \\pattern does: the capture exists inside a branch that the match has
        \\already proved is the right one. Requiring the pattern makes every
        \\payload read dominated by a tag test by construction, instead of by
        \\the programmer's discipline.
    );
    subheading(4, "The three legal readers");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\A switch arm, a pattern test, and a bind-or-diverge binding are the
        \\three shapes that produce a capture. All three bind the payload to a
        \\fresh name that is in scope only where the tag is known.
    );
    code_block(6, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Box: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\area :: (s: Shape) -> s64 {
        \\    if s == {
        \\        case .Circle(r); return 3 * r * r;
        \\        case .Box(w);    return w * w;
        \\        case .Empty;     return 0;
        \\    }
        \\    return -1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\is_unit :: (s: Shape) -> bool {
        \\    if #pattern .Circle(r) = s  return r == 1;
        \\    return false;
        \\}
        \\
        \\radius :: (s: Shape) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Circle(r) := s else { return -1; };
        \\    return r;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% % %\n", area(Shape.Circle(5)), is_unit(Shape.Circle(1)), radius(Shape.Box(9)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\75 1 -1
    );
    table_header(8, "Reader   —   Shape");
    table_row(9, "Switch arm   |   `case .Circle(r);` inside `if s == { ... }`, or the `if #pattern s == { ... }` and `ifx` forms.");
    table_row(10, "Pattern test   |   `if #pattern .Circle(r) = s`, binding `r` for the branch taken.");
    table_row(11, "Bind or diverge   |   `#pattern .Circle(r) := s else { ... };`, binding `r` for the rest of the block.");
    bullet(12, #string,oneline
        \\A qualified pattern is accepted as well as a dotted one:
        \\`if #pattern Shape.Box(q) = s` reads the same as
        \\`if #pattern .Box(q) = s`, because a variant name resolves in the
        \\subject's namespace.
    );
    bullet(13, #string,oneline
        \\An arm that only needs the tag omits the capture: `case .Circle;` is
        \\legal and binds nothing. In a pattern test, `_` serves as the capture
        \\name when the shape must be written but the payload is unwanted:
        \\`if #pattern .Circle(_) = s`.
    );
    bullet(14, #string,oneline
        \\The `else` of a `:=` binding must not fall through. Ending it without
        \\a jump is
        \\`error: the 'else' of a '#pattern ... :=' binding must not return normally`
        \\with `note: it has to be #noreturn - end it with 'return', 'break', 'continue', or a call to a #noreturn function`.
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\The rule is a property of tagged unions, not of user declarations, so
        \\it reaches the built-in option and result types too. `o.Some` on a
        \\`?s64` is
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Some' may only be bound by a pattern match`,
        \\and `r.Ok` on an `E!s64` reports the same thing for `.Ok`.
    );
    subheading(16, "What is exempt");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Three cases are outside the rule, and each is exempt for a reason
        \\that is visible in the code.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\Word  :: union #raw { i: s64; bytes: [8]u8; }
        \\
        \\tag_of :: (s: Shape) -> string {
        \\    if s == { case .Circle; return "Circle"; case .Empty; return "Empty"; }
        \\    return "?";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Shape;
        \\    s.Circle = 7;        // write: selects the variant and stores the payload
        \\    print("%\n", tag_of(s));
        \\    s.Empty;             // bare statement: stores the tag
        \\    print("%\n", tag_of(s));
        \\
        \\    w: Word;
        \\    w.i = 1;
        \\    print("% %\n", w.i, w.bytes[0]);   // raw union: reads are unrestricted
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\Circle
        \\Empty
        \\1 1
    );
    bullet(20, #string,oneline
        \\A write is variant selection, not a read. `s.Circle = 7;` sets the
        \\tag and the payload together, so there is no earlier tag to prove.
    );
    bullet(21, #string,oneline
        \\A bare `s.Empty;` statement is a tag store for a `void` variant, and
        \\is flagged as a setter before the rule runs.
    );
    bullet(22, #string,oneline
        \\A `#raw` union is exempt entirely. It has no tag, and it exists
        \\precisely for C-style reinterpretation, so there is nothing to prove
        \\and nothing to protect.
    );
    bullet(23, #string,oneline
        \\A `void` payload gets no exemption of its own. The statement
        \\`s.Empty;` is a write, but `v := s.Empty;` is a read and is rejected
        \\like any other.
    );
    bullet(24, #string,oneline
        \\Compound assignment is a read followed by a write, so it is rejected:
        \\`s.Circle += 1;` reports
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Circle' may only be bound by a pattern match`.
    );
    subheading(25, "`#enable(allow_union_unwrap)`");
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The gate suspends the requirement to prove the tag statically. It is
        \\a section gate written at the top level of a file: `#enable` applies
        \\to every declaration that starts after it, and `#disable` restores the
        \\rule for everything that follows. Because it is stamped on whole
        \\declarations rather than on statements, it also reaches constant
        \\initializers and top-level `#comptime` blocks, which have no other
        \\escape hatch.
    );
    code_block(27, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Box: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\#enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\peek :: (s: Shape) -> s64 { return s.Circle; }
        \\#disable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", peek(Shape.Circle(5)));
        \\    print("wrong tag read: %\n", peek(Shape.Box(9)));
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\What the gate suspends is the compile-time proof, not the check. At
        \\`-O0` and `-O1` the read lowers to a tag test; the program above
        \\prints `5`, then prints the source position of the read followed by
        \\`union access: expected .Circle, but the value holds one of .Circle | .Box | .Empty`,
        \\and aborts with exit status 134.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\At `-O2` and above the check and its message are omitted. The same
        \\binary reads the payload bits of whatever variant is actually stored
        \\and exits normally.
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\5
        \\wrong tag read: 9
    );
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\This is the sharpest edge in the area: a program that aborts loudly
        \\during development returns a wrong answer silently in a release
        \\build. The gate is for interoperability and for generated code, not
        \\for convenience; a pattern match is checked at every optimization
        \\level.
    );
    bullet(32, #string,oneline
        \\`#enable` is a top-level directive. Inside a procedure body it is
        \\`error: unexpected directive 'enable'`.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\`#disable(allow_union_unwrap)` genuinely restores the rule: a
        \\declaration written after it that reads a payload is rejected again
        \\with the ordinary message.
    );
    subheading(34, "When the tag is already known");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\If the compiler can see which variant the subject holds, the gate
        \\escalates to a compile error rather than emitting a check that could
        \\never pass. A check whose outcome is already decided is not a check;
        \\it is a fault that would fire on every execution.
    );
    code_block(36, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Shape :: union { Circle: s64; Box: s64; Empty: void; }
        \\
        \\A :: Shape.Circle(5);
        \\
        \\#enable(allow_union_unwrap);
        \\main :: () { print("%\n", A.Box); }
        \\#disable(allow_union_unwrap);
    );
    code_block(37, "text", #string
        \\error: this holds .Circle; reading .Box is never valid
        \\note: the tag is known at compile time, so the gate's runtime check would fail on every execution
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\The escalation only applies when the tag is a compile-time fact. A
        \\value that arrives at runtime keeps the runtime check, which is what
        \\the `peek` example above demonstrates.
    );
}

render_section_45 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#from` is a per-variant directive on a tagged union. It marks one
        \\variant as the landing place for its own payload type, and the
        \\compiler generates a `Propagate` instance from that payload type into
        \\the union. A `Disk_Error` can then be widened into the union that
        \\carries it without a hand-written conversion.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The instance exists for `?`. When `?` breaks out of a function whose
        \\carrier residual differs from the operand's, the compiler inserts a
        \\`propagate` call between the two residual types, and the Prelude
        \\lifts a `Propagate` between two error types to a `Propagate` between
        \\the matching `Result_Residual` types. `#from` is therefore what lets
        \\a function returning `App_Error!s64` call one returning
        \\`Disk_Error!s64` and write `?` after it. The propagation machinery
        \\itself is described in `Try`, `?`, and `#try` and in `Propagate` and
        \\Multi-Error Programs.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\App_Error :: union {
        \\    #from parse: Parse_Error;
        \\    #from disk:  Disk_Error;
        \\    Refused: void;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The directive is written before the variant name and does not change
        \\the variant itself: `parse` and `disk` are ordinary variants with
        \\ordinary payloads, matched as `.parse(inner)` and `.disk(inner)`, and
        \\a union may mix `#from` variants with plain ones as `Refused` shows.
        \\What changes is that one extra declaration is injected per marked
        \\variant. The union above generates the equivalent of this, twice:
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\(Disk_Error, App_Error) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: App_Error, value: Disk_Error) -> App_Error {
        \\        out: App_Error;
        \\        out.disk = value;
        \\        return out;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The generated body writes the variant rather than reading it.
        \\`out.disk = value;` is variant selection, which the payload rule
        \\exempts because an assignment chooses the tag instead of trusting it;
        \\see Reading a Union Payload. Writing this instance by hand instead of
        \\marking the variant works and is what `#from` saves.
    );
    subheading(6, "A worked example");
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Parse_Error :: struct { line: s64; }
        \\Disk_Error  :: struct { code: s64; }
        \\
        \\App_Error :: union {
        \\    #from parse: Parse_Error;
        \\    #from disk:  Disk_Error;
        \\    Refused: void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\fetch :: (ok: bool) -> Disk_Error!string {
        \\    if !ok return .Err(.{ .code = 5 });
        \\    return .Ok("42");
        \\}
        \\
        \\decode :: (text: string) -> Parse_Error!s64 {
        \\    if text == "42" return .Ok(42);
        \\    return .Err(.{ .line = 1 });
        \\}
        \\
        \\load :: (ok: bool) -> App_Error!s64 {
        \\    text := fetch(ok)?;
        \\    return .Ok(decode(text)?);
        \\}
        \\
        \\describe :: (value: App_Error!s64) -> string {
        \\    if value == {
        \\        case .Ok(n); return "ok";
        \\        case .Err(e);
        \\            if e == {
        \\                case .parse(inner); return "parse";
        \\                case .disk(inner);  return "disk";
        \\                case .Refused;      return "refused";
        \\            }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% %\n", describe(load(true)), describe(load(false)));
        \\    widened := propagate(App_Error, Disk_Error.{ .code = 9 });
        \\    print("%\n", describe((App_Error!s64).Err(widened)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\ok disk
        \\disk
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\`load` propagates two different error types with the same operator.
        \\`fetch(ok)?` yields a `Disk_Error` residual and `decode(text)?` a
        \\`Parse_Error` residual; both are converted to an `App_Error` residual
        \\by the generated instances before the early return builds the
        \\`App_Error!s64` that `load` declares.
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The generated instance is an ordinary one, so `propagate` may also be
        \\called directly. The witness parameter carries the target type, so
        \\the call is written `propagate(App_Error, value)`, as the last two
        \\lines of `main` show.
    );
    subheading(11, "Without the directive");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\Dropping `#from` from the declaration leaves the variants intact and
        \\removes the conversion. The `?` in `load` then has nothing to convert
        \\the residual with, and the diagnostic lists the two `Propagate`
        \\candidates the Prelude supplies:
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\error: no overload 'propagate' accepts these arguments
        \\note: constraint failed for propagate
        \\note: possible candidates for 'propagate':
        \\candidate: propagate :: (__empty0: Result_Residual(To), value: Result_Residual(From)) -> Result_Residual(To)
        \\candidate: propagate :: (__empty0: To, value: From) -> To
    );
    subheading(14, "What a source type may be");
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\The payload may be any type with a non-zero size: a struct, a scalar,
        \\a `string`. Nothing requires it to implement `Error`; that
        \\requirement belongs to `#derive(Error)`, which is a separate
        \\directive.
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\Small :: union {
        \\    #from n: s64;
        \\    #from s: string;
        \\    Other: void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\give :: (ok: bool) -> s64!bool {
        \\    if !ok return .Err(7);
        \\    return .Ok(true);
        \\}
        \\
        \\wrap :: (ok: bool) -> Small!bool {
        \\    v := give(ok)?;
        \\    return .Ok(v);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Calling `wrap(false)` produces `Small.n(7)` inside the `.Err` arm;
        \\calling `wrap(true)` produces `.Ok(true)`.
    );
    subheading(18, "Rejections");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Four shapes are refused outright, each with its own message. They are
        \\reported at the union declaration, not at the `?` that would have
        \\used the instance.
    );
    table_header(20, "Declaration   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(21, #string,oneline
        \\`union #raw { #from a: E1; }`   |   `error: #from is not defined for
        \\#raw union`
    );
    table_row(22, #string,oneline
        \\`union { #from a: void; }`   |   `error: #from variant 'a' must have
        \\a payload`
    );
    table_row(23, #string,oneline
        \\`union { #from a: E1; #from b: E1; }`   |   `error: #from variant 'b'
        \\duplicates the source type E1`
    );
    table_row(24, #string,oneline
        \\`union { #from a: $T; None: void; }`   |   `error: #from does not yet
        \\support generic unions`
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A raw union has no tag, so there is no variant for the generated body
        \\to select; see Raw Unions. A `void` payload has nothing to convert
        \\from. Two variants with the same source type would produce two
        \\instances with identical heads, and instance selection keys on the
        \\type pair alone, so the pair would be ambiguous at every use.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\`#from` is a union directive. On a struct field it does not reach the
        \\checker at all: `S :: struct { #from a: s64; }` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'from'`.
    );
    subheading(27, "Propagation is one hop");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`Propagate` is not transitive. A union whose `#from` variant carries
        \\another `#from` union gets an instance for that one step only, and a
        \\`?` that would need both steps fails. The candidate list in the
        \\diagnostic shows both generated instances sitting there unused:
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\error: no overload 'propagate' accepts these arguments
        \\note: possible candidates for 'propagate':
        \\candidate: propagate :: (__empty0: High, value: Mid) -> High
        \\candidate: propagate :: (__empty0: Mid, value: Leaf) -> Mid
    );
    note(30, #string,oneline
        \\Chaining requires a hand-written `Propagate` instance for the pair
        \\that is actually needed, or a `#from` variant on the outer union that
        \\names the innermost payload type directly. Adding intermediate
        \\unions does not compose.
    );
    subheading(31, "With a derived `Error`");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\`#from` and `#derive(Error)` are the pair a multi-error program
        \\usually wants: the first makes `?` widen into the union, the second
        \\gives the union the `Error` instance that a `main` returning
        \\`App_Error!void` needs. `#derive(Error)` requires every variant to
        \\carry a payload that itself implements `Error`, which is why the
        \\union below has no payload-less variant.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\App_Error :: union #derive(Error) {
        \\    #from missing: Missing;
        \\    #from number:  Bad_Number;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () -> App_Error!void {
        \\    text := read_config(true)?;
        \\    n := parse(text)?;
        \\    print("n = %\n", n);
        \\    bad := parse("nope")?;
        \\    print("unreachable %\n", bad);
        \\    return .Ok();
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\n = 17
        \\bad number: nope
        \\glosso: program exited with status exit code: 1
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\The second `?` breaks with a `Bad_Number`, the generated instance
        \\widens it to `App_Error`, and the `Termination` instance on `E!void`
        \\reports it through `Error` before turning it into status 1. That
        \\chain is covered in `Error`, `Termination`, and `main`.
    );
}

render_section_46 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`union #raw { ... }` is the untagged union: the C `union`, with C's
        \\rules. It carries no tag word, every variant begins at offset zero,
        \\and any variant may be read at any time regardless of which one was
        \\written last. It exists so that reinterpretation of a value's bits
        \\and foreign declarations that use a C union can be expressed.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\`#raw` is written after the `union` keyword and before the brace, in
        \\any order with the other union directives. Members follow the same
        \\rule as a tagged union's: each ends with `;`.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Word :: union #raw {
        \\    i: s64;
        \\    f: f64;
        \\    bytes: [8]u8;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("size=% align=%\n", size_of(Word), type_info(Word).alignment);
        \\
        \\    w: Word;
        \\    w.f = 1.0;
        \\    print("f=% i=%\n", w.f, w.i);
        \\    print("bytes=% % % % % % % %\n",
        \\        w.bytes[0], w.bytes[1], w.bytes[2], w.bytes[3],
        \\        w.bytes[4], w.bytes[5], w.bytes[6], w.bytes[7]);
        \\
        \\    q := Word.i(258);
        \\    print("q.i=% q.bytes[0]=% q.bytes[1]=%\n", q.i, q.bytes[0], q.bytes[1]);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\size=8 align=8
        \\f=1 i=4607182418800017408
        \\bytes=0 0 0 0 0 0 240 63
        \\q.i=258 q.bytes[0]=2 q.bytes[1]=1
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\`w.i` after `w.f = 1.0` is the IEEE-754 bit pattern of `1.0` read as
        \\an integer, and `w.bytes` is the same eight bytes in memory order,
        \\little end first. Nothing about the read is a conversion: the three
        \\members name three ways of spelling the same eight bytes.
    );
    subheading(5, "Size and alignment");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\The size of a raw union is the size of its largest member, rounded up
        \\to the union's alignment; the alignment is the largest member
        \\alignment. A `void` member contributes nothing. The tagged union with
        \\the same members costs an extra eight-byte tag and takes that tag's
        \\alignment.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Mixed  :: union #raw { a: u8; b: [3]u16; c: u32; }
        \\Tagged :: union      { a: u8; b: [3]u16; c: u32; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("raw    size=% align=%\n", size_of(Mixed), type_info(Mixed).alignment);
        \\    print("tagged size=% align=%\n", size_of(Tagged), type_info(Tagged).alignment);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\raw    size=8 align=4
        \\tagged size=16 align=8
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The largest member is `[3]u16`, six bytes with alignment 2, but `c`
        \\raises the alignment to 4, so the record rounds to 8. Struct layout,
        \\including the field reordering that raw unions have no equivalent of,
        \\is covered in Struct Memory Layout.
    );
    subheading(10, "Construction and reading");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The construction forms are the tagged union's, minus the ones that
        \\only set a tag. A qualified call needs its parentheses; an assignment
        \\to a member stores through it.
    );
    table_header(12, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(13, "`q := Word.i(258);`   |   Qualified call; stores 258 through the `i` member.");
    table_row(14, "`w: Word; w.i = 5;`   |   Member assignment; stores through `i` in place.");
    table_row(15, "`v := w.f;`   |   Member read; reinterprets the stored bytes as an `f64`.");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\The read is the important difference. On a tagged union, naming a
        \\payload member is a compile error that redirects the program to a
        \\pattern match, and the value comes out only through a capture or the
        \\`#enable(allow_union_unwrap)` gate; see Reading a Union Payload. A
        \\raw union is exempt from that rule entirely, because the rule exists
        \\to stop a program from trusting a tag, and a raw union has no tag to
        \\trust.
    );
    note(17, #string,oneline
        \\Nothing checks a raw-union read, at any optimization level. The
        \\tagged-union gate emits a runtime tag check at `-O0` and `-O1` and
        \\drops it at `-O2`; a raw union has no check to drop, so `-O0` and
        \\`-O2` produce identical results. Keeping the discriminant correct is
        \\entirely the program's job.
    );
    subheading(18, "What a raw union gives up");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Every feature that reads the tag is unavailable, and each is rejected
        \\by name rather than silently misbehaving.
    );
    table_header(20, "Construct   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(21, "`if w == { case .i(v); ... }`   |   `error: cannot switch on a raw union (it has no tag)`");
    table_row(22, "`union #raw #derive(Show)`   |   `error: Show derive is not defined for #raw union`");
    table_row(23, "`union #raw #derive(PartialEq)`   |   `error: PartialEq derive is not defined for #raw union`");
    table_row(24, "`union #raw #derive(Error)`   |   `error: Error derive is not defined for #raw union`");
    table_row(25, "`union #raw { #from a: E1; }`   |   `error: #from is not defined for #raw union`");
    bullet(26, #string,oneline
        \\Exhaustiveness has no meaning without a tag, so the checks described
        \\in Exhaustiveness, Guards, and Reachability never apply.
    );
    bullet(27, #string,oneline
        \\`#pattern` against a raw union is not refused at check time. It
        \\reaches lowering, where the missing tag surfaces as an
        \\`error: internal error:` naming the `GetTag` opcode. Read the member
        \\directly instead.
    );
    bullet(28, #string,oneline
        \\`#raw`, `#derive`, and `#magic` are the only union directives.
        \\`union #packed` is `error: unexpected token 'packed'` and
        \\`union #c_call` is `error: unexpected token 'c_call'`; a union has no
        \\field order to pack or to fix.
    );
    bullet(29, #string,oneline
        \\Reflection still works, because it reads the declaration rather than
        \\a value: `variant_count(Word)` is 3 and `variant_name(Word, 0)` is
        \\`i`.
    );
    subheading(30, "Carrying the tag by hand");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\The C idiom a raw union usually appears in is a struct that pairs an
        \\explicit discriminant with the raw payload. The discriminant is an
        \\ordinary enum, so it can be switched on with the exhaustiveness the
        \\raw union cannot offer, and the payload member read in each arm is
        \\the one that arm's tag value promises.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Kind :: enum u8 { As_Int; As_Float; }
        \\
        \\Payload :: union #raw {
        \\    i: s64;
        \\    f: f64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Cell :: struct {
        \\    kind: Kind;
        \\    value: Payload;
        \\}
        \\
        \\render :: (c: Cell) -> string {
        \\    if c.kind == {
        \\        case .As_Int;   return tprint("int %", c.value.i);
        \\        case .As_Float; return tprint("float %", c.value.f);
        \\    }
        \\    return "?";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Cell;
        \\    a.kind = .As_Int;
        \\    a.value.i = 7;
        \\
        \\    b: Cell;
        \\    b.kind = .As_Float;
        \\    b.value.f = 2.5;
        \\
        \\    print("% / %\n", render(a), render(b));
        \\    print("size=%\n", size_of(Cell));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\int 7 / float 2.5
        \\size=16
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\`Cell` is sixteen bytes: a one-byte tag padded out beside the
        \\eight-byte payload. The tagged union
        \\`union { As_Int: s64; As_Float: f64; }` is also sixteen, and it
        \\carries its own discriminant, is exhaustiveness-checked, and refuses
        \\a mismatched read. The hand-rolled pair buys nothing here, which is
        \\the general case: reach for `#raw` when a C declaration or a
        \\deliberate reinterpretation requires it, not to save space.
    );
}

render_section_47 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`?T` is the option type: a value of type `T`, or nothing. It is a
        \\compiler-built-in, injected the way `#Context` is rather than
        \\declared in any `.glo` file, so it is available in every program with
        \\no import. Its definition is exactly this two-variant union:
    );
    code_block(1, "text", #string
        \\Option :: union #magic "option" {
        \\    None: void;
        \\    Some: $T;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\That block is shown for reference and is not writable source. The
        \\name is the compiler's; `?T` is the only spelling a program has, and
        \\writing the identifier produces a parse error that says so:
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\error: the built-in option type is written '?T'; its variants are '.Some(x)' and '.None'
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The compiler owns the declaration for two reasons. The
        \\`#magic "option"` marker is what buys the niche layout described
        \\below, which is the guarantee that non-null `*T` rests on. And the
        \\parser rewrites `?T` into this name, so a program able to redeclare
        \\or shadow the union would leave the syntax pointing at nothing.
    );
    subheading(5, "`?` is a prefix type modifier");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\`?` has no precedence tier of its own. It is annotation syntax that
        \\rewrites to the built-in union, it applies to whatever type follows
        \\it, and it nests. It binds tighter than `!`, so in `?E1!s64` the `?`
        \\takes the error type and the result is a fallible `s64` whose error
        \\is optional.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\E1 :: struct { code: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: ?s64;
        \\    b: ??s64;
        \\    c: ?*s64;
        \\    d: ?E1!s64;
        \\    print("?s64   = %\n", type_name(type_of(a)));
        \\    print("??s64  = %\n", type_name(type_of(b)));
        \\    print("?*s64  = %\n", type_name(type_of(c)));
        \\    print("?E1!s64 = %\n", type_name(type_of(d)));
        \\    print("size_of(?s64) = %\n", size_of(?s64));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\?s64   = Option_int
        \\??s64  = Option_Option_int
        \\?*s64  = Option__int
        \\?E1!s64 = Result_int_Option_E1
        \\size_of(?s64) = 16
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\The mangled names show the tree directly. `Option_Option_int` is
        \\`?(?s64)`, and `Result_int_Option_E1` is a result whose payload is
        \\`s64` and whose error is `?E1`. Operator precedence generally is
        \\covered in Operator Precedence and the 0-20 Scale. One further
        \\property of `?` is that it parses where an expression is expected, so
        \\`size_of(?s64)` works; that does not extend to type syntax in
        \\general, since bracket types are not expressions and
        \\`size_of(?[*]s64)` is `error: unexpected token '['`. Annotate a
        \\variable and use `size_of(type_of(v))` for those.
    );
    subheading(10, "`.Some` and `.None`");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The variants are dotted. `.Some(x)`, `.None`, and a pattern's
        \\`.Some(binding)` all resolve in the subject's namespace, so no
        \\qualifier is needed wherever an expected type is present. Where one
        \\is needed, the sugar supplies it in parentheses.
    );
    table_header(12, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(13, "`a: ?s64 = .Some(3);`   |   Dotted call against the annotation.");
    table_row(14, "`b: ?s64 = .None;`   |   Dotted, no parentheses, for the empty variant.");
    table_row(15, "`c := (?s64).Some(7);`   |   Qualified call where no expected type exists.");
    table_row(16, "`d := (?s64).None();`   |   Qualified empty variant; the parentheses are required.");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The asymmetry in the last two rows is the general union rule, not an
        \\option quirk: a qualified variant is a call and always needs its
        \\parentheses, so `(?s64).None` is `error: member of non-struct`. In
        \\the other direction a bare `.None` with no expected type at all is
        \\`error: enum shorthand needs an enum context`.
    );
    subheading(18, "Reading the payload");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`?T` is an ordinary tagged union as far as the payload rule is
        \\concerned, so `o.Some` is
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Some' may only be bound by a pattern match`.
        \\A switch arm, an `if #pattern` test, or a `#pattern ... :=` binding is
        \\how the value comes out; Reading a Union Payload covers all three and
        \\the gate that suspends the rule.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\lookup :: (key: s64) -> ?s64 {
        \\    if key == 1 return .Some(10);
        \\    return .None;
        \\}
        \\
        \\doubled :: (key: s64) -> ?s64 {
        \\    v := lookup(key)?;
        \\    return .Some(v * 2);
        \\}
        \\
        \\strict :: (key: s64) -> s64 {
        \\    #pattern .Some(v) := lookup(key) else { return -1; };
        \\    return v;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    c := (?s64).Some(7);
        \\
        \\    if a == {
        \\        case .Some(v); print("switch captured %\n", v);
        \\        case .None;    print("switch saw none\n");
        \\    }
        \\    if #pattern .Some(v) = c print("pattern captured %\n", v);
        \\
        \\    print("not: % %\n", !a, !(?s64).None());
        \\    print("propagated: % %\n",
        \\        option_unwrap_or(doubled(1), -1), option_unwrap_or(doubled(2), -1));
        \\    print("bind or diverge: % %\n", strict(1), strict(2));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\switch captured 3
        \\pattern captured 7
        \\not: 0 1
        \\propagated: 20 -1
        \\bind or diverge: 10 -1
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`!o` is the `LogicalNot` instance and means "is none". `?` on an
        \\option is the `Try` instance, whose `Output` is `T` and whose
        \\`Residual` is the one-variant enum `Option_Residual`, so `lookup(key)?`
        \\either yields the `s64` or returns `.None` from the enclosing
        \\function.
    );
    subheading(23, "The null niche");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\When a built-in option or result has exactly two variants, one of
        \\them zero-sized and the other carrying a pointer-like payload, the
        \\tag word is dropped
        \\and the empty variant is represented by the null bit pattern. A valid
        \\pointer is never zero, so zero is a spare pattern the payload can
        \\never occupy, and dropping the tag stays sound. `?*T` is therefore
        \\exactly a `*T` in memory and crosses a C ABI boundary as an ordinary
        \\pointer.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Bits :: union #raw {
        \\    opt: ?*s64;
        \\    raw: u64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 11;
        \\    b: Bits;
        \\
        \\    b.opt = .None;
        \\    print("None bits = %\n", b.raw);
        \\
        \\    b.opt = *n;
        \\    print("Some bits = zero? %\n", b.raw == 0);
        \\
        \\    print("sizes: ?*s64=% *s64=% ?s64=% s64=%\n",
        \\        size_of(?*s64), size_of(*s64), size_of(?s64), size_of(s64));
        \\
        \\    p: ?*s64 = *n;
        \\    q: ?*s64 = .None;
        \\    print("null test: % %\n", p == null, q == null);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\None bits = 0
        \\Some bits = zero? 0
        \\sizes: ?*s64=8 *s64=8 ?s64=16 s64=8
        \\null test: 0 1
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Reading the same eight bytes through a raw union shows that `.None`
        \\is literally address zero. Which types get the niche and which pay
        \\for a tag follows entirely from the pointer-like rule:
    );
    table_header(28, "Type   —   Size   —   Layout");
    table_row(29, "`?*T`, `?[*]T`, `?#fn_ptr(...)`   |   8   |   Niche; `.None` is address 0.");
    table_row(30, "`?bool`, `?u8`, `?enum`   |   16   |   Tag plus payload.");
    table_row(31, "`?[]T`   |   24   |   Tag plus a 16-byte view.");
    table_row(32, "`?string`   |   32   |   Tag plus a 24-byte string descriptor.");
    table_row(33, "`??*T`   |   16   |   The inner takes the niche; the outer's payload is a union.");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\A user-written union never gets a niche, however its variants are
        \\shaped: only the compiler's `#magic "option"` and `#magic "result"`
        \\unions qualify. `union { A: void; B: *u8; }` is sixteen bytes.
    );
    subheading(35, "Widening, and `null`");
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\`*T` widens to `?*T` implicitly, in an assignment and at a call site,
        \\because the niche makes the conversion a no-op. Nothing else widens:
        \\`o: ?s64 = 5;` is `error: got int, expected Option_int`, and the
        \\payload must be written `.Some(5)`. In the other direction `null` no
        \\longer reaches a plain pointer at all, and the diagnostic names `?*T`
        \\as the replacement. Pointers themselves are covered in Pointers and
        \\in Many-Pointers and Null.
    );
    code_block(37, "text", #string
        \\error: 'null' is not a value of *int
        \\note: a plain pointer excludes zero - write ?*int for a pointer that may be absent
        \\note: its absent value is spelled '.None'
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\`p == null` and `p != null` compile on a `?*T` and test for absence.
        \\On an option with a non-pointer payload there is no such operator:
        \\`error: no operator '==' for Option_int, null`.
    );
    bullet(39, #string,oneline
        \\Testing does not refine the type. After `if p != null`, `p.*` is
        \\`error: operator '.*' expects a typed pointer, got Option__int`. A
        \\capture is the only route to the pointer.
    );
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\A declaration cannot use `null` as an option initializer even where
        \\the niche applies: `op: ?[*]s64 = null;` is
        \\`error: null is not a valid Option____int`. Write `.None`.
    );
    subheading(41, "The library surface");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\Everything except the union itself is ordinary Glosso in
        \\`std/Prelude/Option.glo`, and needs no import.
    );
    table_header(43, "Declaration   —   Purpose");
    table_row(44, "`option_none(T: type) -> ?T`   |   The empty value for an explicit type.");
    table_row(45, "`option_some(value: $T) -> ?T`   |   Wraps a value.");
    table_row(46, "`option_is_some(value: ?$T) -> bool`   |   Tag test; `option_is_none` is its negation.");
    table_row(47, "`option_unwrap_or(value: ?$T, fallback: T) -> T`   |   Payload or fallback.");
    paragraph(48, #string,oneline
        \\The instances are `LogicalNot`, `Try`, a hand-written `PartialEq`
        \\(`?T` is `#magic`, so it cannot carry `#derive`), and `Show` for any
        \\`T` that is itself `Show`. Two options compare equal when both are
        \\`.None` or both hold equal payloads.
    );
    note(49, #string,oneline
        \\`Show` for `?T` is a generic instance, and `print` finds a generic
        \\instance's formatter only when some call site has already demanded
        \\that monomorphization. `print("%\n", o)` on its own prints
        \\`{Option_int}`; `print("%\n", show(o))` prints `Some(3)`. The general
        \\rule is in `Show`, `FromString`, and `print` Formatting.
    );
}

render_section_48 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`E!T` is the result type: a `T`, or an error of type `E`. It is the
        \\binary counterpart to `?T` and, like it, a compiler-built-in that no
        \\`.glo` file declares and every program has without an import. Its
        \\definition is this two-variant union:
    );
    code_block(1, "text", #string
        \\Result :: union #magic "result" {
        \\    Ok:  $T;
        \\    Err: $E;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\The sugar is error-first: the LEFT operand of `!` is what `.Err`
        \\carries and the right is what `.Ok` carries, so `Disk_Error!s64` is a
        \\fallible `s64`. The union's own parameters stay payload-first, as
        \\written above; the sugar is what swaps, not the declaration, which is
        \\why `type_name(Disk_Error!s64)` is `Result_int_Disk_Error`.
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Error-first is the order that reads. A signature ends on the type its
        \\success path hands back, which is the type the caller is looking for,
        \\and the error type sits next to the `!` that introduces it. It is
        \\also the order Zig's `E!T` and Haskell's `Either e a` use, and it is
        \\what makes right associativity the natural rule for a repeated `!`.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\As with `?T`, the compiler's name for the union is not writable.
        \\`E!T` is the only spelling and the identifier is a parse error:
    );
    code_block(5, "text", #string
        \\error: the built-in result type is written 'E!T'; its variants are '.Ok(x)' and '.Err(e)'
    );
    subheading(6, "Precedence and associativity");
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\`!` binds looser than every prefix type modifier, so `?`, `*`, `[]`,
        \\`[..]`, and `[*]` all stay glued to whichever operand they were
        \\written next to. It is right-associative, which is what error-first
        \\order asks for: `E1!E2!T` is `E1!(E2!T)`, each `!` wrapping the one
        \\payload in another result.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\E1 :: struct { a: s64; }
        \\E2 :: struct { b: s64; }
        \\User :: struct { id: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: E1!?*User;
        \\    q: ?E1!User;
        \\    r: E1!E2!User;
        \\    s: E1![]u8;
        \\    t: []u8!E1;
        \\
        \\    print("E1!?*User  = %\n", type_name(type_of(p)));
        \\    print("?E1!User   = %\n", type_name(type_of(q)));
        \\    print("E1!E2!User = %\n", type_name(type_of(r)));
        \\    print("E1![]u8    = %\n", type_name(type_of(s)));
        \\    print("[]u8!E1    = %\n", type_name(type_of(t)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\E1!?*User  = Result_Option__User_E1
        \\?E1!User   = Result_User_Option_E1
        \\E1!E2!User = Result_Result_User_E2_E1
        \\E1![]u8    = Result___u8_E1
        \\[]u8!E1    = Result_E1___u8
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The mangled name is `Result_<payload>_<error>`, so each line can be
        \\read straight off:
    );
    table_header(11, "Written   —   Reading");
    table_row(12, "`E1!?*User`   |   A fallible optional pointer: error `E1`, payload `?*User`.");
    table_row(13, "`?E1!User`   |   A result whose ERROR is optional, not an optional result.");
    table_row(14, "`E1!E2!User`   |   `E1!(E2!User)`, from right associativity.");
    table_row(15, "`E1![]u8`   |   Error `E1`, payload `[]u8`.");
    table_row(16, "`[]u8!E1`   |   Error `[]u8`, payload `E1`; the slice modifier stayed on the left.");
    note(17, #string,oneline
        \\`!*` lexes as a single operator token, so `E1!*User` is
        \\`error: unexpected token '!*'` and `E1!**User` is
        \\`error: unexpected token '!**'`. Separate them with a space, as
        \\`E1! *User`, or write the pointer through a named alias. `!?` and
        \\`![` do not have this problem, because `?` is emitted alone and `[`
        \\is not an operator character.
    );
    subheading(18, "`.Ok` and `.Err`");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The variants are dotted and resolve in the subject's namespace, and
        \\the qualified form parenthesises the type exactly as an option's
        \\does. `.Ok()` with no argument is how an `E!void` reports success.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Bad :: struct { text: string; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Bad!s64 = .Ok(3);
        \\    b: Bad!s64 = .Err(.{ .text = "nope" });
        \\    c := (Bad!s64).Ok(7);
        \\    d := (Bad!s64).Err(Bad.{ .text = "x" });
        \\
        \\    if a == {
        \\        case .Ok(n);  print("switch ok %\n", n);
        \\        case .Err(e); print("switch err %\n", e.text);
        \\    }
        \\    if #pattern .Err(e) = b print("pattern err %\n", e.text);
        \\
        \\    print("is_ok: % % % %\n", result_is_ok(a), result_is_ok(b),
        \\        result_is_ok(c), result_is_ok(d));
        \\    print("not: % %\n", !a, !b);
        \\    print("unwrap_or: % %\n", result_unwrap_or(a, -1), result_unwrap_or(b, -1));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\switch ok 3
        \\pattern err nope
        \\is_ok: 1 0 1 0
        \\not: 0 1
        \\unwrap_or: 3 -1
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`!r` is the `LogicalNot` instance and means "is err", the mirror of
        \\`!o` on an option. Payload reads obey the same rule as any tagged
        \\union: `r.Ok` is
        \\`error: tagged-union payload '.Ok' may only be bound by a pattern match`,
        \\and the legal readers are the ones in Reading a Union Payload.
    );
    subheading(23, "`Try` and `?`");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`E!T` carries a `Try` instance whose `Output` is `T` and whose
        \\`Residual` is `Result_Residual(E)`, a one-field struct holding the
        \\error. `x?` on a result therefore yields the payload or returns early
        \\with the error already wrapped in the enclosing function's carrier.
        \\Returning a bare `Output` from a result-returning function is also
        \\legal: `from_output` wraps it, so `return x + y;` means
        \\`return .Ok(x + y);`.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Bad_Number :: struct { text: string; }
        \\
        \\number :: (text: string) -> Bad_Number!s64 {
        \\    parsed := from_string(s64, text);
        \\    if parsed == {
        \\        case .Some(n); return .Ok(n);
        \\        case .None;    return .Err(.{ .text = text });
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\sum_two :: (a: string, b: string) -> Bad_Number!s64 {
        \\    x := number(a)?;
        \\    y := number(b)?;
        \\    return x + y;
        \\}
        \\
        \\report :: (r: Bad_Number!s64) -> string {
        \\    if r == {
        \\        case .Ok(n);  return tprint("ok %", n);
        \\        case .Err(e); return tprint("bad %", e.text);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", report(sum_two("12", "30")));
        \\    print("%\n", report(sum_two("12", "3x")));
        \\    inner := #try { number("4")? + number("5")? };
        \\    print("try block: %\n", report(inner));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\ok 42
        \\bad 3x
        \\try block: ok 9
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`?` needs an enclosing Try boundary. Outside one it is
        \\`error: postfix '?' requires a Try-returning function or an enclosing #try block`;
        \\`#try { ... }` supplies a boundary for a single expression, as the
        \\last two lines show. The operator itself is documented in `Try`, `?`,
        \\and `#try`.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\When the propagated error type differs from the enclosing function's,
        \\the compiler inserts a `propagate` call between the two residuals.
        \\The Prelude lifts any `Propagate(From, To)` between error types to
        \\the corresponding `Result_Residual` pair, and `#from` variants on a
        \\union generate those instances; see `#from` Variants and `Propagate`
        \\and Multi-Error Programs.
    );
    subheading(29, "A result-returning `main`");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\`$E!void :: instance Termination where Error(E)` is what lets `main`
        \\return a result: `.Ok` becomes status 0, and `.Err` reports the error
        \\through its `Error` instance and becomes status 1. The constraint is
        \\real, so an `E` with no `Error` instance gives
        \\`error: main return type Result_void_E does not implement Termination`.
        \\Only `E!void` qualifies; a general `E!T` and `?T` do not.
    );
    code_block(31, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Missing :: struct { path: string; }
        \\
        \\Missing :: instance Error {
        \\    report_error :: (e: Missing) -> void {
        \\        error_report_line("missing", e.path);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\load_setting :: (ok: bool) -> Missing!s64 {
        \\    if !ok return .Err(.{ .path = "config" });
        \\    return .Ok(17);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () -> Missing!void {
        \\    n := load_setting(true)?;
        \\    print("n = %\n", n);
        \\    m := load_setting(false)?;
        \\    print("unreachable %\n", m);
        \\    return .Ok();
        \\}
    );
    code_block(32, "text", #string
        \\n = 17
        \\missing: config
        \\glosso: program exited with status exit code: 1
    );
    subheading(33, "Size and niche");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\A result is a tag word plus the larger of its two payloads. With
        \\`Small :: struct { c: s64; }`, `size_of(Small!s64)` is 16; with
        \\`Bad :: struct { text: string; }`, whose `string` descriptor is 24
        \\bytes, `size_of(Bad!s64)` is 32. A result takes the niche under the
        \\same rule as an option, and either side may be the zero-sized one.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Nil :: struct { }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: *s64!Nil;
        \\    b: []u8!Nil;
        \\    print("% %\n", size_of(type_of(a)), size_of(type_of(b)));
        \\    print("% %\n", type_name(type_of(a)), type_name(type_of(b)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\8 24
        \\Result_Nil__int Result_Nil___u8
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\`*s64!Nil` has a zero-sized `.Ok` payload and a pointer `.Err`
        \\payload, so the tag goes and the whole result is one pointer word.
        \\`[]u8!Nil` does not qualify because a view is not pointer-like, and
        \\pays the tag.
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\`E!T` has no `Show` instance at all. Passing a result to `print` is
        \\rejected with: variadic arg type `Result_int_Bad` does not implement
        \\`Show` in call `print`. Match on the result and format the arms.
    );
    bullet(39, #string,oneline
        \\`E!T` has no `PartialEq` either, and unlike `Show` the omission is
        \\not caught in the checker: `a == b` on two results passes checking
        \\and fails during code generation with
        \\`error: Invalid operand types for ICmp instruction`.
    );
    subheading(40, "The library surface");
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\As with the option, everything but the union itself is ordinary
        \\Glosso in `std/Prelude/Result.glo`, available with no import. The
        \\three `*_error` functions map, apply, and bind over the error side
        \\rather than the payload, which is what a program rewriting one error
        \\type into another needs.
    );
    table_header(42, "Declaration   —   Purpose");
    table_row(43, "`result_ok(E: type, value: $T) -> E!T`   |   Success, with the error type named.");
    table_row(44, "`result_err(T: type, error: $E) -> E!T`   |   Failure, with the payload type named.");
    table_row(45, "`result_is_ok(value: $E!$T) -> bool`   |   Tag test; `result_is_err` is its negation.");
    table_row(46, "`result_unwrap_or(value: $E!$T, fallback: T) -> T`   |   Payload or fallback.");
    table_row(47, "`map_error(value: $E1!$T, f: (E1) -> $E2) -> E2!T`   |   Rewrites the error type.");
    paragraph(48, #string,oneline
        \\`ap_error` and `bind_error` complete that group, and
        \\`Result_Residual :: struct { error: $E; }` is a real, writable type,
        \\which is what makes a hand-written `Propagate` between two residuals
        \\possible.
    );
}
